#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# grid_lines

#% text
# `grid_lines` is an option of graphics and plots: `none`, `automatic` (at the ticks), or `[x_lines, y_lines]`, each `automatic`, `none` or a list of positions or `[position, directives]` pairs. Graphics and plots draw no grid lines unless asked: `grid_lines: automatic` (or `true`) gives lines at the ticks, on a logarithmic axis at the powers of ten with fainter lines at their multiples.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
plot(sin(x), [x, 0, 2pi], grid_lines: automatic)
#% output
#= image("grid_lines.files/grid_lines-1.svgz")

#% cell
plot(x^2, [x, 0, 3], grid_lines: [[1, 2], [[4, dashed]]])
#% output
#= image("grid_lines.files/grid_lines-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# On a logarithmic axis:

#% cell
log_plot(exp(x), [x, 0, 10], grid_lines: automatic)
#% output
#= image("grid_lines.files/grid_lines-3.svgz")


#% text
# A colour given as `"#rrggbb"`:

#% cell
plot(x, [x, 0, 1], grid_lines: [[[0.5, "#e0115f"]], none])
#% output
#= image("grid_lines.files/grid_lines-4.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `ticks`, `frame`, `epilog`
